"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:10,662\nMemory systems and automaticity\n\n4\n00:00:13,462 --> 00:00:15,670\nAfter surgery to stop his seizures,\n\n5\n00:00:15,801 --> 00:00:17,554\na man known as H.M.\n\n6\n00:00:17,859 --> 00:00:21,100\ncould no longer form new memories of facts or events.\n\n7\n00:00:21,574 --> 00:00:27,325\nBrenda Milner gave him a hard task: trace a star while watching only its mirror image.\n\n8\n00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,106\nOver three days, he got steadily better.\n\n9\n00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:33,873\nYet each day, he had no memory of ever doing it before.\n\n10\n00:00:34,043 --> 00:00:35,883\nMemory is not one system.\n\n11\n00:00:36,229 --> 00:00:39,008\nDeclarative memory holds facts and events.\n\n12\n00:00:39,363 --> 00:00:40,697\nYou can say what you know,\n\n13\n00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:42,596\nand it depends on the hippocampus.\n\n14\n00:00:42,986 --> 00:00:45,979\nProcedural memory holds skills and habits.\n\n15\n00:00:46,248 --> 00:00:47,902\nIt runs without awareness,\n\n16\n00:00:47,992 --> 00:00:51,065\nand leans on the basal ganglia and cerebellum.\n\n17\n00:00:51,235 --> 00:00:53,597\nA classic study pulled the two apart.\n\n18\n00:00:54,069 --> 00:00:57,452\nPeople learned to predict the weather from patterns on cards.\n\n19\n00:00:57,791 --> 00:01:00,443\nAmnesic patients learned the task normally,\n\n20\n00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:02,438\nbut barely remembered the training.\n\n21\n00:01:02,958 --> 00:01:04,832\nPatients with Parkinson's disease,\n\n22\n00:01:04,852 --> 00:01:06,869\nwhich affects the basal ganglia,\n\n23\n00:01:06,967 --> 00:01:08,236\nshowed the reverse.\n\n24\n00:01:08,595 --> 00:01:10,040\nThey remembered the session,\n\n25\n00:01:10,096 --> 00:01:11,632\nbut didn't learn the task.\n\n26\n00:01:11,802 --> 00:01:16,962\nWith practice, a skill shifts from slow and effortful to fast and automatic.\n\n27\n00:01:17,464 --> 00:01:20,000\nThat frees working memory for the next layer.\n\n28\n00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:22,941\nA fluent reader stops sounding out letters,\n\n29\n00:01:23,069 --> 00:01:25,148\nand starts thinking about the argument.\n\n30\n00:01:25,318 --> 00:01:27,022\nBut automatic has a cost.\n\n31\n00:01:27,388 --> 00:01:31,793\nExpert golfers putted worse when told to attend to each step of the stroke,\n\n32\n00:01:31,993 --> 00:01:33,616\nwhile novices putted better.\n\n33\n00:01:33,988 --> 00:01:36,558\nAnd experts lose sight of what was once hard.\n\n34\n00:01:37,095 --> 00:01:43,701\nFuture teachers with more advanced math training misjudged which algebra problems students would find difficult.\n\n35\n00:01:44,169 --> 00:01:46,889\nMany expected equations to be easiest.\n\n36\n00:01:47,257 --> 00:01:49,814\nFor students, they were often the hardest.\n\n37\n00:01:49,984 --> 00:01:50,992\nOne caution.\n\n38\n00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:53,408\nFluency is not understanding.\n\n39\n00:01:53,804 --> 00:01:56,326\nA student can run a procedure automatically,\n\n40\n00:01:56,346 --> 00:01:58,395\nand still not know why it works,\n\n41\n00:01:58,490 --> 00:01:59,895\nor when to use it.\n\n42\n00:02:00,065 --> 00:02:00,836\nFor teaching.\n\n43\n00:02:01,283 --> 00:02:04,406\nDecide which pieces students should make automatic,\n\n44\n00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:05,963\nlike notation, core facts,\n\n45\n00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:07,200\nor routine steps.\n\n46\n00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:10,274\nDrill those deliberately, spaced over time.\n\n47\n00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:12,318\nKeep the reasoning in view,\n\n48\n00:02:12,338 --> 00:02:14,535\nso fast steps stay tied to meaning.\n\n49\n00:02:14,985 --> 00:02:16,208\nAnd when you explain,\n\n50\n00:02:16,228 --> 00:02:19,460\nslow down and unpack the steps you no longer notice.\n\n51\n00:02:19,630 --> 00:02:22,054\nPractice moves a skill out of the spotlight.\n\n52\n00:02:22,468 --> 00:02:24,919\nThat frees the mind for what comes next.\n\n"
